My twitter feed just absolutely exploded with new threads that I have bookmarked.  I'm going to run the ingest again.  Should I worry about the script picking up duplicates that have already been ingested?

No, you're covered — just don't add --force. The pipeline has three separate layers of dedup:

The primary gate checks whether a local report file already exists at .nfl\reports\twitter-bookmarks\<date>-<author>-<tweet-id>.md before doing anything else, and skips it as "already-processed" if so — that's what prevented reprocessing during your recent runs. Even if something slipped past that, the Supabase insert into research_intel_notes checks by URL and logs "already exists for this URL — skipping duplicate insert" rather than inserting a second row (we saw this happen live during your --force run). And the vault_notes write is an upsert keyed on path, so even a re-write just overwrites the same row instead of duplicating.

So just run npm run twitter:bookmarks:cron (or the plain live command) normally — it'll walk through the whole new batch, skip everything it's already touched, and only actually ingest what's genuinely new. The one thing to keep in mind given the feed explosion: the 8-day recency window is still active, so anything in that flood that's older than about a week will get filtered as stale rather than ingested — which is the behavior you asked for, just worth knowing if you notice fewer new items than you bookmarked.